.TH "HTMLELEMENT.CONTAINERTIMINGIGNORE" "3JS" "October 26, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
HTMLElement.containerTimingIgnore \- HTMLElement: containerTimingIgnore
property
.SH SYNOPSIS
The \f[B]\f[CB]containerTimingIgnore\f[B]\f[R] property of the
\f[CR]HTMLElement\f[R] interface excludes the element and its
descendants from contributing to the timing of any ancestor container
root in the Container Timing API.
.PP
The \f[CR]containerTimingIgnore\f[R] property reflects the value of the
\f[CR]containertimingignore\f[R] attribute.
.SH VALUE
A boolean value.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Logging the value of \f[CR]containerTimingIgnore\f[R]
In this example, adding the \f[CR]containertimingignore\f[R] attribute
to a \f[CR]<div>\f[R] element excludes it from its ancestor
container\(cqs timing.
.IP
.EX
<\f[B]section\f[R] containertiming=\(dqhero\(dq>
  <\f[B]div\f[R] containertimingignore id=\(dqignored\(dq>
    \f[I]<!\-\- ... \-\->\f[R]
  </\f[B]div\f[R]>
</\f[B]section\f[R]>
.EE
.PP
We can then read the property and log its value:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] el = document.getElementById(\(dqignored\(dq);
console.log(el.containerTimingIgnore); \f[I]// true\f[R]
.EE
.PP
For examples of how to use the Container Timing API to get timing
information, see \f[CR]PerformanceContainerTiming\f[R].
.SH SEE ALSO
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]PerformanceContainerTiming\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]containertimingignore\f[R] HTML attribute
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]HTMLElement.containerTiming\f[R]
